00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  two weeks of climate protests begin as  extinction rebellion take to london  streets demanding an end to investment  in fossil fuels  flash floods and record-breaking  rainfall hit the u.s state of tennessee  and could hydrogen hold the key to green  steel  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  extinction rebellion have kicked off two  weeks of protests by using a giant pink  table to block roads around leicester  square in central london the  environmental campaign group say the  disruption will continue until the  government agrees to stop all new fossil  fuel investment immediately  skies ashna hurinag is at the protest  and has the latest well this marks the  first day of two weeks of action that  we're walking through at the moment a  crowd i'd say about a thousand or so  moving from trafalgar square going up  towards those big institutions up in the  city one of those that's joined us on  this march today is sophie sophie thanks  so much for talking to me talk to me  about why you're here the importance of  taking action today um i'm here because  i want to listen and uh respect the  planet i believe that we need to cut  down on fossil fuel usage and stop  extracting fossil fuels i'm also here as  a mum  and i'm here for the future of my kids  and i'm here for the future of  everybody's children  we all have a right to be here  and we need to believe that trees have a  right to be here that the bees have a  right to be here and we need to protect  everybody we need to tell stories about  the future and those stories have got to  be hopeful and at the moment they're not  they're absolutely depressing and as a  storyteller myself i'm here because i  feel that  i need to shout to people that there is  a possibility there is a future there's  an imaginative a collective imagination  right here on the streets of london  today and it's going to carry on for the  next two weeks why is it important to  you that the fossil fuel investment from  some of these big companies stops now  why is that the immediate action that's  being called for i think it's essential  because it's so short-sighted it's a  small-term gain for a long-term pain and  that's a real problem for the future of  our planet thank you very much well the  march is likely will continue throughout  the day there is no such plan as such of  where those protesters will end up but  we don't do know that they will be  occupying some of those big spaces in  london but particularly those financial  institutions the bps the shells of this  world they of course have their hq's  right here in the city of london and  that is where the protesters want to  make their voices heard the most  and you can read more about the  extinction rebellion protests the  controversy surrounding them and what  the government is doing to reduce  investment in fossil fuels on our  website skynews.com  climate  parts of tennessee have been devastated  by flooding after record-breaking levels  of rainfall hit the u.s state more than  20 people have died and numerous homes  were swept away after flash floods this  weekend well the area was hit by lines  of storms for hours resulting in a  record amount of moisture a scenario  scientists have warned may become more  frequent due to global warming well up  to 17 inches of rain fell in less than  24 hours shattering the state's record  for one day rainfall by more than three  inches according to the national weather  service now flash flooding in the city  of waverly 60 miles from nashville took  out roads telephone lines and even swept  away homes  while nbc correspondent sam brock is  there so sam just how unusual is the  extremity of this weather and what's the  situation like there now  anna it's good to be with you extremely  unusual it's not that this part of the  united states doesn't see flooding it  certainly does especially this time of  the year what's unusual is that there  was a flash flood warning and then  neighbors as they describe it to me saw  homes storage units and swimming pools  just floating through their  neighborhoods the creek where this all  came from at least in this part of  waverly anna is over my shoulder right  here it looks nice and docile right now  there was so much rain water as you very  articulately put just a second ago the  17 inches which is a historic level that  it came up from over there washed over  the lot that i'm standing on went across  the street and reached the porch level  of the homes next door one 69-year-old  woman that i interviewed said she had to  be picked up and carried away the water  on the other side past where those trees  are went out onto the highway a quarter  mile away that's pretty extreme we say  that it moved homes over here this is  the foundation of what once was a home  there that home is actually if you look  up right over my shoulder twirled around  360 degrees and now sitting on the bank  of a river next to that there's a series  of homes all juxtaposed together one of  them anna came from across the street so  you talk about the historic precedence  of this kind of thing back in 2010 there  were bad floods here in this part of  middle tennessee but from what i'm told  here the neighbors say they said it was  a thousand year plan then  we're 10 years removed and now we're  seeing something that is even worse is  that due to global warming is it the  amount of moisture that is in the  atmosphere right now hard to say but we  are seeing more events in the united  states and certainly across the globe  germany comes to mind where there are  flood events that are breaching levels  that had not previously been thought  possible but that's what's happening  right now anna  sam thank you well the flight in  tennessee aren't the only extreme  weather events in the united states at  the moment there's been flooding in new  york and new jersey after storm honoree  made landfall on sunday over 140 000  homes lost power and deluges of rain  closed bridges swamped roads and left  some people stranded in their vehicles  meanwhile in california officials are  struggling to contain wildfires that  have destroyed hundreds of buildings and  forced thousands of people to leave  their homes more than a dozen large  wildfires have burned roughly 2  300 square miles of grass brush and  forest lands that are exceptionally dry  due to years of drought  well all this comes as kamala harris is  in singapore the u.s vice president is  visiting the region to discuss issues  including climate change the two  countries are launching the u.s  singapore climate partnership to develop  new environmental standards and  collaborate on projects to expand clean  energy infrastructure development and  improve sustainability  steel is a crucial part of  infrastructure building and development  around the world but producing it has a  huge climate impact well now a way of  making the construction material without  using coal is being developed which  could be crucial to reducing emissions  in the future here's why the solution is  needed  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  well let's bring in fourstein de la  salle who's a director of the energy  transitions commission uh welcome to you  so just how green is the green steel  that's being trialled at the moment and  is it all purely down to how the steel  plants are powered  it is not only how the steel plant are  powered it's the whole production  process  from the mine to  the final step of refining the steel  that are important to the carbon eyes  and so the technology that has been  tested by ssab now  at an initial scale is very exciting  because it decarbonizes the whole  process  and how close are we to getting green  steel to market then  well we are a step closer now that  multiple technologies are getting  tested at scale by multiple companies  out there most of the major steel  manufacturers  are investing and have announced for  first of a kind plants to test new  technologies that produce zero carbon  steel the problem now is really to  deploy those technologies at scale the  announcements until now have been of a  handful of projects and we have more  than  500 plants around the world that need to  shift to those new technologies so  deployment is going to take time and  it's going to be a major investment  challenge well yes interesting you say a  major investment challenge is there a  cost to moving to green alternatives  there is today uh the green steel that  is produced by by an ssab or by other  producers is at least 50 percent more  expensive than the high carbon steel at  this point in time and if the targets  are to be met at cop 26 the climate  summit at this autumn how important is  it that steel production is decarbonized  it is uh really important that we have  first steps this year as you probably  know we need to reduce uh global  emissions by 50 by 2030 and that's  really the big  challenge for cop 26 this year  steel will be in the harder to abate  sectors to decarbonize it will take more  time to decarbonize a sector like steel  but if the technologies are not  commercially available by 2030 then we  won't be able to deploy them at scale in  the 2030s so what we expect from copter  26 this year is to see the whole steel  value chain the steel manufacturers but  also the the iron ore miners the energy  providers the buyers of steel in the  automotive sector in construction and  equipment uh really joining forces to  find the business models that will work  in the future  foreign thanks very much indeed  now in the latest of our series of  climate diaries we're meeting alex  stewart who co-founded an eco-conscious  luggage brand aiming to reduce the  amount of single-use plastic in airports  and make travel items more sustainable  beginning of 2018 i had almost a meter  of small intestine removed i've been  fairly unwell for about six months  afterwards just spent  some time in intensive care on a  ventilator in an induced coma  and i came to the point where i was like  well if i didn't survive  what would be the three regrets number  one i've never done my own thing or set  up my own business number two i'd never  been in love and number three i'd never  seen england win the world cup  so with the first point in mind i set  about creating 195.  i was fairly fairly unaware of the  plastic problem that we faced on the  planet  um and i think after watching blue  planet two followed by  reading about early pioneers in the uk  people like emily penn and lucy siegel i  really felt compelled to try and make a  positive change we estimate that over  151 million of these single-use clear  plastic bags are distributed at uk  airports every year  to help tackle this we've redesigned the  wash bag to incorporate a reusable and  detachable clear pouch that's designed  to airport security standards research  suggests we've got really good at  switching from single-use plastics  things like coffee cups water bottles  bags for life at the supermarket but  maintaining that routine when you're  traveling is difficult and we want to  help people on the move  [Applause]  well that's everything from us for today  next time how a byproduct from making  paper could be a climate friendly  solution to energy storage you can find  out more tomorrow here on the daily  climate show see you then  [Music]

